KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ function type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.type]/1, [dcl.fct]/5: two template-ids are the same when their
type arguments are the same type; decltype(cb) and void(int) are.  A
written function type template argument keeps its parameters in parsed
form (cpp_declaration, with the declarator part such as `&' or `*'
separate from the base type) while decltype(cb) is a converted code type
(parameters as `parameter'), so the instances differ
(tag-W<(parameter(...))->(void)> vs tag-W<(cpp_declaration(...))->(void)>)
and the primary template is selected.  Normalizing either form changes what
the partial-specialization matcher compares, which relies on the parsed
form.  (decltype(cb) itself used to be a pointer type; see
cpp11_decltype_of_function_name.)  `same<void(int), void(int)>' and
pointers to functions work.  Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18
accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
